# Swift Package Manager
.build/
.swiftpm/
*.xcodeproj
*.xcworkspace
Package.resolved
# ...EXCEPT the two apps that have REMOTE dependencies to pin.
#
# The criterion is not "is it an app" but "does it resolve anything remotely".
# Apps/macOS, Apps/linux and Packages/TailscreenKit depend only on local
# `path:` packages, so SwiftPM writes no Package.resolved for them at all —
# there is nothing to lock and committing one would commit an empty file. The
# two below pull swift-cross-ui (and Apps/windows also swift-winui) over the
# network, so their graphs are the ones that can move underneath us.
#
# Pinning is right for these specifically because they are LEAF executables:
# nobody consumes them and re-resolves against their own constraints, which is
# the situation where a committed lockfile would be presumptuous. For the
# library packages the default ignore stays correct.
#
# Both apps pin swift-cross-ui to the same revision in their manifests, and
# that agreement is currently held only by two hand-maintained literals.
# Committing both lockfiles makes a divergence show up in a diff.
#
# For Apps/windows it is also load-bearing for CI: the Windows build cache is
# keyed on this file's hash, so while it was ignored, `swift package resolve`
# had to run BEFORE the cache could be restored purely to produce the key —
# 61s of a warm job spent generating something that could have been checked
# out. With it committed, restore goes first and resolve is a no-op against
# the restored checkouts.
!Apps/windows/Package.resolved
!Apps/linux-gtk/Package.resolved

# macOS
.DS_Store
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# Build artifacts
Tailscreen
.envrc

# Intermediate iconset from `make icon`; only the final .icns is committed.
Apps/macOS/Resources/*.iconset/
e2e/.native-run/
e2e/.native-bin/
